      "textEn": "World—Of the world; Sindhum—The ocean; Ati-duraram—Impossible to cross; Uttirishroih—Those who desire to cross; Na—Not; Anyaah—Someone else; Plavah—Ship; Bhagavatah—The Lord; Purusha-uttamasya—Of the supreme being; Lila-\nStories—Stories of divine plays; Rasa—Taste of divine play; Nishevanam—Serving; Antarena—In addition to this; Punasah—For men; Bhaveet—May; Vividah – Various; Dukkah – Of material sorrow; Daavah – By fire; Arditasya—Afflicted..\n\nFor a person suffering from the fire of countless sorrows and desiring to cross the insurmountable ocean of the world,\nthere is no other suitable boat besides practicing devotion to the divine taste of the stories of the Lord's pastimes. Meaning: Even though it is impossible to fully describe the divine plays of God, still,\nwith even a little praise, one can be saved from the unbearable suffering of the world. The fever of existence can only be removed by the name of God\nand by the medicine in the form of divine plays, which is adequately described in the Srimad Bhagavatam.\nPuranasamhita meṭāmṛṣiṇāryaṇoṁavyayaḥ. Narada Praha Krishna Dwaipayanaya Saha. 41. Meaning of the word\n\nPurana—All the Puranas; Samhitam—The essential Samhita; Etam—This; Rishi—The great sage; Narayanah—Narayana;\nAvyayah—Indelible; Naradayah—From Narada Muni; Purah—In ancient times; Praah—Said; Krishna-Dvaipayana—Krishna Dvaipayana\nFrom Vedavyasa; Sah—He, Narada. Many days have passed, this essential code of all the Puranas, Achyuta Nar-Narayan Rishi had said to Narad,\nwho then said it to Krishna Dwaipayana Vedavyasa. Sa vai mahyam maharaj bhagwanvadarayanah. Ima Bhagavati, pleased, the complete collection of the Vedas. 42. Meaning of the word\n\nSaha—He; Vai—Indeed; Maiyam—From me, from Shukadeva Goswami; Mahaaraaja—Oh King Parikshit; Bhagavaan—Of the Supreme God; Shaktiapaavatar—Powerful incarnations; Badarayana—Srila Vyasadeva; Imaam—This; Bhagavatim—To the Bhagavata scripture; Preetah—Being pleased;\nSamhitam—The Samhita; 