—मर जाने पर, मुक्तअवस्था में;\nजीवः—जीवात्मा; ब्रह्म—अपनी आध्यात्मिक स्थिति; सम्पद्यते—प्राप्त कर लेता है; पुनः—फिर से।\n\nजब घड़ा टूट जाता है, तो घड़े के भीतर का आकाश पहले की ही तरह आकाश बना\nरहता है। उसी तरह जब स्थूल तथा सूक्ष्म शरीर मरते हैं, तो उनके भीतर का जीव अपना\nआध्यात्मिक स्वरूप धारण कर लेता है।",
      "textEn": "Like a seed, apart from the body, like a fire. 3. Meaning of the word\n\nNa bhavishyasi—will not be; bhutva—having become; tvam—you; putra—children; pautra—grandsons; adi—etcetera; roop-vant—one who has form; beej—seed; akku ra—likewise; ankur— sprout; vat—like; saman— similar; deha-adeha— to the physical body and its accessories;\nvyadhikarah—separate; yatha—as; analah—fire (from wood). You will not be reborn as your sons and grandsons, just as a seed sprouts\nand creates a new seed again. But you are entirely different from the physical body and its instruments, just as fire is different from its fuel. Meaning: Sometimes people see this dream that they are reborn as the son of their son.\nSo that they can live continuously in the same physical family. As it is said in the Shruti-Mantra—Pita\nPutrena Pitramaan Yonionau—The father sees the father in his son because he can be born in the form of his grandson.\nThe purpose of Shrimad Bhagwat is spiritual salvation, not to maintain the delusion of physical identity for a long time. This is explicitly stated in this verse. Dreams, as if a head is cut off, fivefold, by the self. From whom the body sees, that soul is immortal. 4. Meaning of the word\n\nSwapne—in a dream; Yatha—in the manner; Shirah—of someone's head; Hodham—to be cut; Panchatva-adi—made of five elements;\nThe state of being and other situations; Atmanah—of oneself; Swayam—self; Yasmat—because; Pashyati—sees;\nDehasya—of the body; Tatah—therefore; Atma—soul; Hi—certainly; Ajah—unborn; Amarah—immortal..\n\nIn a dream, a person can see his own head being cut off, and he can understand\nthat his soul stands apart from the experience of the dream. Similarly, while awake, a person can see that his body is the result of five physical elements. Therefore, it is believed\nthat the real soul is separate from that body which it sees, and it is unborn and immortal. The sky, which is divided into different parts, was like this in the beginning.\nAnd the embodied being, having become one with Brahman, again attains the Supreme Being. 5. Meaning of the word\n\nDecreases—in the pot; separated—on breaking; ghat-akashah—the sky inside the pot; akashah—sky; syat—may be; yatha—as; pura—as before; evam—in the same way; dehe—in the body; mrite—after dying, in a liberated state;\nJeevah—soul; Brahma—one's spiritual state; sampadayate—is attained; punah—again. When the pot breaks, the sky inside the pot remains as the sky was before. Similarly, when the gross and subtle bodies die, then the being within them\ntakes on a spiritual form."
