      "textEn": "Suta said.\n\nHaving thus spoken, the Lord, the best of the gods. Worshiped by the world, along with the monks. 8. Meaning of the word.\n\nSuta said—Shri Suta Goswami said; Iti—Thus; Uktah—Said; Tam—To him; Anugrahya—By giving permission;\nBhagwan—The mighty saint; Badarayaniha—The son of Badarayana Vedavyasa, Shukadeva; Jagam—Went away; Bhiksubhih—With monks; Sakam—Together; Nar-Devena—By the king; Pujitah—Worshipped. Sut Goswami said: Upon being persuaded in this manner, Srila Vyasadeva's son\ngave permission to King Parikshit. Thereafter, the king and the sages present there,\nbeing worshiped, Shukdev departed from that place. Parikshitapi rajarshiratmanamatmanamatmana. Samadhaya param dadhyavaspanndasyauryatha taruha. 9. Prakule bahirishyasino gangakul udadhmukhah.\nThe great yogi, absorbed in Brahman, free from doubt. 10>. Meaning of the word\n\nParikshit—Maharaj Parikshit; Api—Even further; Raj-rishi—A great king with a noble nature; Atmani—Within one's own\nSpiritual identity; Atmanam—One's own mind; Atmana—With one's own intellect; Samadhaya—By keeping; Param—In the supreme; Dadhyau—Meditated; Asu—One's own life; Yatha—As; Taru—Tree; Prak-kule—Towards the east; Barhishi—On the darbha grass; Asinah—Sitting; Ganga-kule—On the banks of the Ganges; Udak-mukha—Facing north; Brahma-bhutah—Completely realizing one's true identity; Maha-yogi—Highest Yogi;\nNisangah—free from all material attachments; Chhinna—broken; Sanshayah—all doubts..\n\nThen Maharaj Parikshit sat on the eastern-facing Darbha seat on the banks of the Ganges River\nand turned north himself. After attaining Yogasiddhi, they experienced complete self-\nrealization, and they were free from all material attachments and doubts.\nSadhu\nThe king, with his pure intellect, stabilized his mind within his soul and\nbegan to meditate on the Supreme Brahman. Their life force stopped moving, and they became rooted like a tree. Takshaka sent the Brahmins, angered by the Brahmin's anger. Hantuk