अधमः—नीच।\n\nजब राजा जनमेजय ने तक्षक को यज्ञ-अग्नि में प्रवेश करते नहीं देखा तो उन्होंने",
      "textEn": "Vrushuha, Pushpavarshani, Vibudha, Sadvadinah. 15. Meaning of the word\n\nDev—of the gods; Dundubhi—of the drums; Nedh—started playing; Gandharva-Apsaras—of the Gandharvas and Apsaras; Jagu—\nsang; Vavarishu—of the rain; Pushpa-varshani—of the rain of flowers; Vibudha—of the gods; Sadhu-vadinah—while praising. In the heavens, the drums began to beat, and the celestial musicians and nymphs sang. The gods showered flowers and spoke words of praise. Meaning: Although all the learned men, including the deities, were initially mourning,\nthey soon realized that a great soul had returned to the abode of the Lord. This was indeed\nthe cause of joy. Janamejaya, having heard of his father's death, the tiger-eater.\nAs the angry snake attacks, so do the twice-born (Brahmins) together. 16. Meaning of the word:\n\nJanamejaya - Parikshit's son Janamejaya; Swa-Pitaram - to his father; Shrutva - having heard; Takshaka - by Takshaka; Bhakshitam - having been devoured; Yatha - in due manner; Ajahav - started performing the Havan; Sankruddha - extremely angry; Nagan - to the snakes; Satre -\nIn the great Yagya; Saha - with; Dwijaih - Brahmins.Hearing that Takshak had bitten and killed his father badly, Maharaj,\nJanamejaya became extremely angry and had a huge Yagya performed by the Brahmins, in which they offered all the snakes of the world\ninto the Yagya fire. The great animals are being burned in the sacrificial fire of the serpent. The tiger, terrified by the sight of the arrow, sought refuge. 17. Meaning of the word\n\nSerpent-session—in the serpent sacrifice; Samidha—lit; Agnau—in the fire; Dahyamanan—being burned; Maha-urgana—great;\nTo the serpents; Drishta—by seeing; Indram—to Indra; Bhaya-vignaan—extremely disturbed; Takshakah—Takshak; Sharanam—Refuge;\nFor; Yayau—went.\nWhen Takshak saw that even the most powerful snakes were being burned in the blazing fire of that snake sacrifice,\nhe became agitated with fear and reached Indra for refuge. There, King P