      "textEn": "It was Lord Krishna. Jupiter wanted that the young king Janamejaya should see these things from a spiritual perspective.\nSee. By the fire of the serpent, by the lightning, by the hunger, by the diseases, O king. The five aggregates of clinging arise from karmic fetters. 26. Meaning of the word\n\nSerpent—serpents; thief—thieves; fire—fire; lightning—lightning; hunger—hunger; thirst—thirst;\nDisease—disease; etc.—other causes; king—O king; five—death; attains—obtains; creature—\nBound creature; enjoys—enjoys; arisen—before the past action; karma—desirable karmic fruit; that—that. \"When a living being is killed by snakes, thieves, fire, lightning, hunger, disease, or any other cause,\nhe experiences the consequences of his own past actions.\"\n\nMeaning: According to Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur, King Parikshit was not bearing the fruit of his past deeds. Because of being a great devotee, they were taken to the abode of God by God himself. Therefore, this is the royal institution, the conduct of the king.\nThe serpent Anagaso is burned by the people who desire it. 27. Meaning of the word\n\nTherefore,\nSatram—to the sacrifice; Idam—this; Rajan—Oh King; Sansthayit—should be stopped; Abhicharikam—\ndone with the intention of causing harm; Sarpaah—snakes; Anagasah—innocent; Dagdhaah—burned; Janaih—by people;\nDishtam—fortune; Hi—indeed; Bhujyate—is enjoyed. \"Therefore, O King, this Yagya (sacrificial ritual) which has been started with the intention of causing harm to others,\nhas been stopped. Many innocent snakes have already been burned to death. Undoubtedly, all\nindividuals should bear the unseen consequences of their past deeds.\"\n\nMeaning: Here, Brihaspati accepts that although the serpents appeared innocent,\nthrough the system of God, they were also being punished for their previous corrupted deeds. Suta said.\nThus said, and then, thus said, the great sage, the words of the eyes.\nI worship the speech, which is superior to the serpent. 28. Meaning of the word.\n\nSoota said—Soot Goswami said; Iti—Thus; Uktah—It was said; Sah—He (Janamejaya) said; Tatha—Thus; Iti—Thus.\nTathastu—So be it; Aah—Said; Maha-rishih—Of the great sages; Manayan—Respectfully; Vachah—Word; Sarpa-satrat—From the serpent sacrifice;\nUparatah—While stopping; Pujayam aas—To worship; Vakpatim—Lord of speech, Brihaspati. Suta Goswami said: Upon being advised thus, Maharaja Janamejaya replied,\n“So be it”. Respecting the words of the sage, they stopped the snake sacrifice and then"
