      "textEn": "Param—Supreme; Padam—Position; Vaishnavam— Belonging to Lord Vishnu; Aamananti— They chant the name; Tat—That; Yat—Which; Na Iti Na—Not this;\nIti—Thus; Analyzing; Atat—That which is external; Utsrisrikkhavah— Desirous of renunciation; Visrijya—Renouncing; Daaratmyam— Inferiority; Ananya— Unique; Sauhridaah— Their affection; Hrida— Within their hearts; Upaguhya— By embracing them; Avasitam— Captured; Samadhaiha— In meditation; By those who are in Samadhi. Those who want to abandon all those things through the prohibition of external things, which are actually\nnot true, they regularly move towards the supreme position of Lord Vishnu. They\nRenouncing petty materiality, they bestow their love upon the Supreme Brahman within their hearts.\nAnd in still meditation, they embrace that supreme truth.\nMeaning: 'Yannneti Netityaduttsrikshvah' is indicative of the method of discrimination that is prohibitive in terms, by which\na person engaged in the search for the ultimate truth excludes all those things which are futile, superficial,\nand relative. Throughout the world, people have gradually abandoned the legitimacy of political, social, and even religious truths,\nbut in the absence of Krishna consciousness, they remain deluded and\nhysterical. But here it is clearly said—the other path, the Vaishnava, they believe. Those who are fully\ndesirous of knowledge should not only abstain from useless things but also understand the spiritual truth of\nPadam Vaishnavam. The position is indicative of the position and abode of God,\nwhich only those people can understand who renounce petty materialism and uniquely\nacquire the position of exclusive love for God.\nSuch unique love is not narrow-minded,\nor communal, because all beings, by being within God, are automatically sanctified\nwhen they serve the Supreme Being. This is the method of serving God and all living beings to the highest extent.\nThis is the science of Krishna consciousness, the education of which is given in the entire Shrimad Bhagavatam. They go to that supreme abode of Vishnu. I, in this, am not of wealth, nor am I of any kind of body. 33. Meaning of the word\n\nThey—they; This—this; They come to know; Vishnu's—of Lord Vishnu; That—who; Supreme—supreme; Position—position;\nI—I; Mine—mine; Thus—in this way; Poverty—poverty; Not—not; For whom—for whom; Body—body; House—house; Based—based. Such devotees are able to understand the supreme spiritual position of Lord Vishnu because they are not contaminated by the thoughts of \"I\"\nand \"mine\", which are based on the body and home. Ativadanti na vamananyeta kanchana. Why should one, taking refuge in the body, harbor enmity towards anyone? 34. Lexicon"
